// Wonder Lab astronomy data. Generated by tools/build_extras.py
// Observed first. No star is born, formed or dies anywhere in here —
// nobody has watched that happen. See .work/BRIEF-astronomy.md.
// 112 facts across 8 sections.
const ASTRO = [{"id":"sun-1","section":"sun","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"The sun is about 864,000 miles across (1.39 million km). Line up Earths edge to edge along that width and you would need 109 of them. It is not a special star to look at from far away. It is just the one close enough to measure carefully.","more":"Measuring it is ordinary geometry. You know how far away the sun is, because radar bounced off nearby planets pins down the distances in the solar system. You can measure how wide it looks in the sky. Distance plus apparent width gives you real width."},{"id":"sun-10","section":"sun","cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"When the sun throws out a burst of particles and they reach Earth, the sky over the far north and far south lights up green and red. That is the aurora. During the great storm of 1859 it was reported from the Caribbean, thousands of miles outside its usual range.","tryit":"Space weather forecasts are public. Search for the NOAA aurora forecast, and when the number called Kp climbs to 7 or higher, it is worth going somewhere dark and looking north."},{"id":"sun-11","section":"sun","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"The sun is not solid, and it does not turn all in one piece. Track the same sunspot across the face day after day. The equator comes round in about 25 days. The regions nearer the poles take closer to 35. Nothing rigid can do that."},{"id":"sun-12","section":"sun","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Helium was found in the sun before anyone found it on Earth. On 18 August 1868 Jules Janssen saw an unexplained yellow line in sunlight during an eclipse. Norman Lockyer saw the same line, and named the new element after the Greek word for the sun. It turned up on Earth in 1882.","more":"Spread light out into a spectrum and every element leaves its own pattern of lines. Nobody had seen that particular yellow line in any laboratory. So it belonged to something nobody had ever handled. It turned up in volcanic gas at Vesuvius fourteen years later. It is the same helium that is in party balloons."},{"id":"sun-13","section":"sun","cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis says the lights in the sky were set for signs and seasons, and for days and years. The sun is what a day is measured against, and one trip of the Earth around it is what a year is. Every calendar on every wall is counting the sun."},{"id":"sun-14","section":"sun","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"A small hole makes a picture. Sunlight passing through a pinhole in a card lands on the ground as a small round disc, and that disc is an image of the sun itself. It is safe, because you are looking at the card and not at the sun.","tryit":"Push a pin through a piece of card, stand with your back to the sun, and let the light fall onto a second card. During a partial eclipse that little disc turns into a crescent. Gaps between leaves in a tree do exactly the same thing for free, and a tree will scatter hundreds of crescents across the sidewalk."},{"id":"sun-2","section":"sun","cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"Never look straight at the sun, and never point binoculars or a telescope at it. The light burns the retina, and the retina has no pain nerves, so nothing hurts while the damage is being done. Eclipse glasses that meet the ISO 12312-2 standard are thousands of times darker than sunglasses.","more":"Damage from staring at the sun is called solar retinopathy. There is no treatment for it. Sunglasses are not eclipse glasses, no matter how dark they look. Wearing eclipse glasses and then looking through a telescope is worse than useless. The telescope concentrates the light and burns straight through the filter."},{"id":"sun-3","section":"sun","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"On 1 September 1859 Richard Carrington was sketching sunspots. A patch of the sun flared white while he watched. About 17 hours later, telegraph wires across Europe and North America sparked. Operators on the Boston-to-Portland line unhooked their batteries. They kept sending messages on the current the sky was pushing through the wires.","more":"It is called the Carrington Event, after the man who happened to be at the eyepiece. Aurora were reported as far south as the Caribbean. Because the whole thing was written down by hundreds of people at once, it is one of the best-documented things the sun has ever done."},{"id":"sun-4","section":"sun","cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"On 24 December 2024 NASA's Parker Solar Probe passed 3.8 million miles (6.1 million km) above the sun's surface, moving at roughly 430,000 miles per hour (690,000 km/h). Nothing built by people has ever gone faster or closer. It radioed home two days later to say it was fine."},{"id":"sun-5","section":"sun","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"The sun is about 400 times wider than the moon and it sits about 400 times farther away. Those two numbers cancel out, so from Earth the two discs look almost exactly the same size. That is the entire reason a total solar eclipse is possible.","more":"There is no rule of nature that makes the numbers match. They just do, here, for us. On a total eclipse the moon covers the bright disc so precisely that the sun's faint outer glow, the corona, becomes visible with the naked eye."},{"id":"sun-6","section":"sun","cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"People have been drawing sunspots since 1610, when telescopes were brand new. In 1843 Heinrich Schwabe, after seventeen years of near-daily counting, noticed the number rises and falls in a repeating pattern. Rudolf Wolf then dug through old observatory records and fixed the cycle at about eleven years. The counting has never stopped.","more":"This is one long chain of ordinary people writing down a number. Galileo used sunspots to show that the sun turns. Schwabe was an amateur, counting spots in his spare time while actually hunting for a planet he never found. The count he started is still kept today."},{"id":"sun-7","section":"sun","cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"Sunlight takes about 8 minutes 20 seconds to cross the 93 million miles (150 million km) to Earth. Both halves of that are measured: the distance by bouncing radar off nearby planets, and the speed of light in laboratories. The sunlight on your hands left the sun before you sat down."},{"id":"sun-8","section":"sun","cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"Nobody has been inside the sun and nothing ever will be. The figure given for its center, about 27 million °F (15 million °C), is worked out from the way the whole sun rings and wobbles, plus what its light contains. The surface temperature is measured directly: about 10,000 °F (5,500 °C).","more":"The sun shakes very slightly, all over, all the time, and those movements can be watched on its surface. How they travel through the sun depends on what the inside is like. Reading them is a bit like telling a cracked bell from a whole one by the sound. It is a reasoned answer, not a look inside."},{"id":"sun-9","section":"sun","cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"A sunspot looks black in a photograph, but it is not black. It is simply cooler than the surface around it, roughly 6,300 °F against about 10,000 °F. It looks dark the way a candle flame looks dark when you hold it up in front of a floodlight."},{"id":"stars-1","section":"stars","cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"The color of a star is a thermometer reading. Split its light into a spectrum and you can measure how hot the surface is. Betelgeuse, the orange star on Orion's shoulder, comes out near 3,500 K. Rigel, the blue-white one at his foot, is around 12,000 K. Red is cool, blue is hot.","more":"This is not guesswork about what a star is made of or what it is doing. Put a poker in a fire and it goes dull red, then orange, then white as it heats. Same physics, pointed at a light source and read off with an instrument.","tryit":"On a winter evening find Orion. Look at the star at the top left of the four corners, then the one at the bottom right, and go back and forth a few times. Betelgeuse is clearly orange. Rigel is white with a blue edge. You are seeing a temperature difference with bare eyes."},{"id":"stars-10","section":"stars","cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"Stars twinkle and planets mostly do not. A star is so far off that it arrives as a single point of light, and any wobble in the air shoves that whole point sideways. A planet arrives as a tiny disc, which is many points at once, and the wobbles cancel each other out.","more":"Next clear night, hunt for something bright that is not flickering. Steady light, especially low in the sky, is usually a planet. Compare it against a star at the same height above the horizon and the difference shows up in a couple of seconds."},{"id":"stars-11","section":"stars","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The brightness scale runs backwards, and it is Hipparchus's fault. He sorted the stars into six ranks and called the brightest ones stars of the first magnitude. The system stuck, so bigger numbers now mean fainter stars. Sirius comes out at minus 1.46. The faintest an ordinary eye can catch is about 6.","more":"Each step of one magnitude is a brightness ratio of about 2.5. Five steps is therefore a factor of 100. That was chosen deliberately in the 1850s. It fits the old eyeball rankings onto a proper measured scale without throwing the ancient numbers away."},{"id":"stars-12","section":"stars","cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"Algol loses about two thirds of its light and gets it back, over and over, every 2 days and 21 hours. John Goodricke, a deaf teenager in York, timed the cycle and in 1783 said plainly what it meant: a second star was passing in front of the first.","more":"He was 18 when he started watching. He was 19 when the Royal Society gave him its Copley Medal for it. He died at 21. He was also right. Algol is a pair of stars circling each other, edge-on from where we sit. The dip you can follow with your own eyes is one crossing the other."},{"id":"stars-13","section":"stars","cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"A European spacecraft called Gaia spent years repeating the parallax measurement Friedrich Bessel first managed in 1838. It measured positions and parallaxes for more than 1.8 billion stars. Look up at almost anything you can see with your eyes. Its distance is now a measured number, not an educated guess.","more":"Gaia had to pin down angles smaller than a thousandth of an arcsecond. That is about the width of a human hair seen from ten miles away. It managed it by scanning the entire sky over and over. Each time round, it compared where every star appeared to sit."},{"id":"stars-14","section":"stars","cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Under a genuinely dark sky, an ordinary eye can pick out about 9,100 stars across the whole sphere. Only half of them are above you at any moment. Psalm 147:4 says of God, \"he determines the number of the stars; he gives to all of them their names.\" Somebody counted first.","more":"The figure comes from catalogues of everything brighter than magnitude 6.5, which totals 9,096 stars over the entire sky. From one spot, with haze thickening near the horizon, most people actually see two or three thousand. Genesis 1:16 covers the lot in a single short clause: \"and the stars\"."},{"id":"stars-2","section":"stars","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Sirius is the brightest star in the night sky, magnitude minus 1.46, and it is bright for two reasons at once. It genuinely puts out more light than the sun, and it is close, 8.6 light-years off, one of our nearest neighbors. Brightness in the sky always mixes those two things together.","more":"Sirius has a companion, Sirius B, which is about ten thousand times fainter and only turns up in a good telescope. The two swing around each other once every fifty years, and that orbit has been tracked, drawn and timed by people at the eyepiece. In winter, follow the three stars of Orion's belt down and to the left and they point straight at Sirius."},{"id":"stars-3","section":"stars","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Hold a finger up and close one eye, then the other. The finger jumps against the far wall. That jump is parallax, and it is how the distance to nearby stars is measured. Earth's two eyes are its position in January and its position in July, and it is the star that does the jumping.","more":"The angle involved is tiny, but once you have measured it, plain geometry gives you the distance. Nothing has to be assumed about the star itself. Of all the ways of getting a distance in astronomy, this is the only one that is pure measurement.","tryit":"Hold your thumb at arm's length and blink one eye, then the other. Now bring it in to a foot away and try again. The nearer it is, the further it jumps. That is exactly the relationship astronomers use, just with a much longer gap between the two eyes."},{"id":"stars-4","section":"stars","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Friedrich Bessel was the first person to measure the distance to a star. In 1838 he published a measured parallax for the star 61 Cygni: 0.314 of an arcsecond, roughly the width of a coin seen from ten miles away. That put 61 Cygni about 10 light-years off. The modern figure is 11.4, so he was close.","more":"Bessel was not alone for long. Struve and Henderson published parallaxes for Vega and Alpha Centauri within about a year of him. John Herschel called Bessel's result \"the greatest and most glorious triumph which practical astronomy has ever witnessed\". Before 1838 nobody had a measured distance to any star at all."},{"id":"stars-5","section":"stars","cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"The nearest star to the sun is Proxima Centauri, 4.24 light-years away, and you cannot see it. It is far too faint for the naked eye, around magnitude 11. Robert Innes found it in 1915 by comparing photographs of the same patch taken years apart and spotting one dot that had shifted.","more":"Its distance is known by parallax, the measuring trick Friedrich Bessel first used on a star in 1838. It was measured independently by Joan Voute in 1917 and Harold Alden in 1928. The nearest star to us is invisible without a telescope, and the brightest star in the sky is not the nearest one. There is no rule that says close means bright."},{"id":"stars-6","section":"stars","cat":"weird","kind":"worked","text":"Orion is not a group of stars. It is a group of directions. The three belt stars look evenly spaced in a neat row, but they are nothing like neighbors: Alnitak is put at around 1,260 light-years and Alnilam at nearly 2,000. Move far enough sideways and the hunter falls apart.","more":"Those two distances are worked out rather than measured with geometry. They come from comparing how bright each star looks with how bright it is taken to be. So an assumption is baked into the numbers. The point survives whatever the numbers do. A constellation is a pattern seen from one particular place. That place is here."},{"id":"stars-7","section":"stars","cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"On 4 July 1054 Chinese astronomers wrote down a guest star near the southern horn of Taurus. It had not been there before. It was bright enough to see in broad daylight for 23 days, stayed visible at night for 653 days, then faded out. No European record of it survives.","more":"The dates are exact because the observers logged them. First seen 4 July 1054. Last seen 17 April 1056. In that same spot there is now a faint tangle of glowing gas. Charles Messier put it at the very top of his catalog as M1. Everyone calls it the Crab Nebula."},{"id":"stars-8","section":"stars","cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"Ian Shelton was working at an observatory in Chile on 23 February 1987. He developed a photographic plate and found a bright star in the Large Magellanic Cloud. It was not on any of his earlier plates. He walked outside and checked with his own eyes. It was there. It was the first supernova visible without a telescope since 1604.","more":"Hours before anyone saw the light, detectors buried underground in Japan, in Ohio and in the Caucasus each caught a short burst of neutrinos. About two dozen particle hits, inside a few seconds. It is the only time that has ever happened."},{"id":"stars-9","section":"stars","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Betelgeuse dimmed, and people noticed with their eyes. Between late 2019 and February 2020 it lost roughly two thirds of its brightness. Hubble and the big telescopes in Chile watched the whole thing. A cloud of dust thrown off the star had drifted across about a quarter of its face.","more":"The Very Large Telescope in Chile photographed the star's disk going lopsided as the patch darkened. In the months before, Hubble tracked dense material moving outward through the star's atmosphere. The cloud ended up more than a hundred million miles across. Betelgeuse was back to its usual brightness by the end of 2020."},{"id":"giants-1","section":"giants","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Jupiter is 88,846 miles across at the equator (142,984 km). That is eleven Earths side by side. It carries more than twice as much material as every other planet in the solar system put together. It also turns faster than any of them. One full rotation takes 9 hours 55 minutes.","more":"Spinning that fast has a visible effect. Jupiter is measurably squashed. Across the poles it is only 83,082 miles. That is about 5,800 miles narrower than across the equator. You can see the flattening in a decent telescope."},{"id":"giants-10","section":"giants","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Saturn is the only planet lighter than water for its size. Its average density is 0.69 grams per cubic centimeter and water is 1, so given a big enough ocean it would float. Both halves of that sum are measured: its width from photographs, its mass from how it swings its moons."},{"id":"giants-11","section":"giants","cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"On 14 January 2005 the Huygens probe parachuted down through Titan's orange haze and landed. No spacecraft has ever landed farther from Earth. It kept transmitting for 72 minutes from the surface, sent back the sound of the wind, and photographed channels cut by flowing liquid. The liquid there is methane, not water."},{"id":"giants-12","section":"giants","cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"Enceladus is a small icy moon of Saturn with four long cracks near its south pole, nicknamed the tiger stripes. Jets of water vapor and ice grains fire out of them into space. Cassini flew straight through the spray and analysed it, which is a spacecraft tasting a moon without ever landing on it."},{"id":"giants-13","section":"giants","cat":"speed","kind":"found","text":"Neptune receives a tiny fraction of the sunlight Earth does and still has the fastest winds anywhere in the solar system. Voyager 2 flew past on 25 August 1989 and clocked them near 1,200 miles per hour (2,000 km/h) around a storm called the Great Dark Spot. That spot has since disappeared."},{"id":"giants-14","section":"giants","cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis has the lights set in the expanse for signs and seasons. Jupiter and Saturn are two of them, both bright enough to see without any equipment. On 21 December 2020 they came within a tenth of a degree of each other, a fifth of the moon's width, the closest since 1623."},{"id":"giants-2","section":"giants","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"The Great Red Spot is a storm people have measured, on and off, since the 1800s. Around 1879 its long axis came out near 24,000 miles. Hubble now measures about 10,250 miles. That is not a story about the distant past. It is a shrinking that happened while people watched.","more":"Amateur astronomers noticed the shrinking speeding up in 2012. Hubble now measures the spot every year. Nobody knows why it is shrinking. That it is shrinking is written down in numbers."},{"id":"giants-3","section":"giants","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Saturn's rings reach out to about 175,000 miles from the planet (282,000 km) and are typically only around 30 feet thick (10 m). Cassini's instruments found them to be over 99% water ice. Seen exactly edge-on from Earth, something that wide thins out and all but vanishes."},{"id":"giants-4","section":"giants","cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"Cassini spent thirteen years at Saturn and was then destroyed on purpose. From April to September 2017 it made 22 weekly dives through the roughly 1,200-mile gap between the planet and the rings. On 15 September 2017 it flew into Saturn's atmosphere and burned up, so it could never crash into a moon.","more":"Saturn has moons where the conditions are worth protecting. So the mission was ended in a way that kept the spacecraft off them. It could not drift down onto one carrying Earth bacteria. It sent data all the way in. It stopped when its thrusters could no longer hold the antenna pointed at Earth."},{"id":"giants-5","section":"giants","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Uranus is tipped over by about 98 degrees. It rolls around the sun on its side instead of spinning upright. Its rings circle its equator, so instead of lying flat like Saturn's they stand up, and from Earth they can look like a target aimed straight at you."},{"id":"giants-6","section":"giants","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Neptune was found with a pencil before anyone looked. Uranus was not moving quite as it should, so Urbain Le Verrier worked out where an unseen planet would have to sit to tug it that way. On 23 September 1846 Johann Galle aimed a telescope there and found Neptune within one degree.","more":"John Couch Adams in England had been working on the same problem and got a similar answer. An international argument about credit followed. The sequence is not in dispute. The calculation came first. The telescope came second."},{"id":"giants-7","section":"giants","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"On 7 January 1610 Galileo pointed a homemade telescope at Jupiter and saw three little stars in a line beside it. On 13 January a fourth turned up. Night after night they shifted, and he concluded they were going round Jupiter. He published it in March 1610 and called them the Medicean Stars.","tryit":"Steady a pair of binoculars against a wall or a fence and look at Jupiter. Those same four moons show up as tiny points strung out in a line. Look again the next night and they will have moved. You are seeing exactly what Galileo saw."},{"id":"giants-8","section":"giants","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"On 9 March 1979 Linda Morabito was checking a navigation photograph from Voyager 1. Those are the lowest-priority kind of image. She noticed an odd bulge off the edge of Jupiter's moon Io. It was a plume from an erupting volcano, about 170 miles tall. It was the first active volcano ever seen anywhere but Earth."},{"id":"giants-9","section":"giants","cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"Europa is wrapped in cracked ice, and something under that ice bends Jupiter's magnetic field in a way that fits a salty ocean. Nobody has drilled through. The ocean is a conclusion drawn from a magnetic reading and the pattern of the cracks. It has never been seen.","more":"Nobody has been to Europa's surface, let alone under it. A magnetic reading and a pattern of cracks are what there is. That is enough to make a salty ocean the best answer available, and not enough to call it a thing anyone has observed."},{"id":"rocky-1","section":"rocky","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Mercury is the closest planet to the sun and it is not the hottest. Venus is. Venus sits much farther out and still runs at roughly 870 °F (465 °C), hot enough to melt lead, because a thick blanket of carbon-dioxide air traps the heat. Mercury has almost no air at all.","more":"Venus's air is about 96% carbon dioxide. The pressure at the surface is around 93 times Earth's. That is the same squeeze you would feel about 3,000 feet under the ocean. Between the pressure and the heat, it is the most hostile surface any spacecraft has ever reached."},{"id":"rocky-10","section":"rocky","cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"A small box called MOXIE rode on the Perseverance rover. Martian air is nearly all carbon dioxide, and MOXIE pulled oxygen out of it. It made about 4 ounces (122 grams) of oxygen across 16 runs. The last run finished on 7 August 2023, and the oxygen was at least 98% pure. A small amount, and it worked."},{"id":"rocky-11","section":"rocky","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Mars has two small moons, both spotted by Asaph Hall in August 1877. Phobos goes round Mars in 7 hours 39 minutes, which is three times in a single Martian day. It laps the planet underneath it, so from the ground Phobos rises in the west and sets in the east."},{"id":"rocky-12","section":"rocky","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Mercury is the smallest planet, 3,032 miles across (4,879 km). Two moons beat it. Ganymede, which goes round Jupiter, and Titan, which goes round Saturn, are both wider than Mercury is. Counting as a planet is about what you orbit, not about being big."},{"id":"rocky-13","section":"rocky","cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"Gravity on Mars pulls at about 38% of Earth's. A kid who weighs 70 pounds here would tip the scales at about 26 pounds there, and could jump getting on for three times as high. That pull is not guessed at: it is measured by watching orbiting spacecraft speed up and slow down."},{"id":"rocky-14","section":"rocky","cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis has the lights in the sky set for signs and seasons. Venus keeps that appointment very plainly. It is the brightest thing in the sky after the sun and the moon. It hangs in the evening for months on end. Then it swaps over and does the same before dawn.","tryit":"Look low in the west just after sunset, or low in the east before dawn, depending on the month. Venus is the one that does not twinkle and outshines every star by a wide margin. A calendar or a stargazing app will tell you which half of the year you are in."},{"id":"rocky-2","section":"rocky","cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Mercury has the widest temperature swing of any planet. Where the sun strikes it is about 800 °F (430 °C). On the dark side it drops to about -290 °F (-180 °C). Mercury has essentially no atmosphere. Nothing carries heat from the lit side round to the unlit one."},{"id":"rocky-3","section":"rocky","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Venus turns backwards, and it turns very slowly. One rotation takes 243 Earth days. One trip around the sun takes 225. Its day is longer than its year. If you could stand on the surface and see through the clouds, the sun would come up in the west."},{"id":"rocky-4","section":"rocky","cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"The Soviet lander Venera 13 reached the surface of Venus on 1 March 1982. It scraped up a soil sample and analyzed it. It sent home the first color photographs ever taken from the surface of another planet. It was built to survive about 30 minutes down there. It lasted 127.","more":"The photographs show flat orange rock under a sky the color of weak tea. Nothing has landed on Venus since, so those pictures are still the only color views anyone has of that surface."},{"id":"rocky-5","section":"rocky","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Olympus Mons on Mars stands about 16 miles high (25 km). It spreads roughly 370 miles across (600 km), wide enough to cover Arizona. It is about three times the height of Everest. The slope is so broad and so gentle that standing on it, you might not realize you were on a mountain."},{"id":"rocky-6","section":"rocky","cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"Voyager 1 was 3.7 billion miles out (6 billion km) on 14 February 1990. It turned its camera back toward home and photographed Earth. Earth came out smaller than a single pixel. It was a speck sitting in a band of scattered sunlight. Thirty-four minutes later the cameras were switched off for good.","more":"The picture was Carl Sagan's idea, and it took years of arguing to get it taken. Pointing the camera near the sun risked the instrument. The picture had no scientific value either. He called the result a pale blue dot. Everyone you have ever met is somewhere in that speck."},{"id":"rocky-7","section":"rocky","cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Nobody had photographed the whole Earth in a single frame until 7 December 1972, when the Apollo 17 crew, outbound to the moon, caught it lit from edge to edge. Africa and the Antarctic ice cap are both in the shot. It became one of the most reproduced photographs there has ever been."},{"id":"rocky-8","section":"rocky","cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"Nobody has been to the middle of the Earth. The deepest hole drilled, at Kola in Russia, went 40,230 feet down: 7.6 miles (12.3 km), under a fifth of one percent of the way. What gets said about the core is worked out from how earthquake waves bend, and where they refuse to travel.","more":"Certain earthquake waves cannot pass through liquid. There is a zone on the far side of the planet from a big quake where those waves simply never arrive. The shape of that shadow is the evidence for a liquid outer core. It is a conclusion drawn from a gap in the readings, and it should be said that way."},{"id":"rocky-9","section":"rocky","cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"Mars is rusty orange all day and then the sunset goes blue. Fine iron-rich dust in the thin air scatters blue light forward, so it bunches up around the sun while the rest of the sky stays butterscotch. Curiosity filmed it from Gale Crater on 15 April 2015.","more":"It is the exact reverse of Earth, where the sky is blue and the sunset is red. On a day when there is less dust aloft, the Martian blue is fainter, which is a neat check on the explanation: less dust, less effect."},{"id":"deepsky-1","section":"deepsky","cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Hanging below Orion's belt is a fuzzy patch you can see with your eyes on a clear winter night. That is the Orion Nebula, a glowing cloud of gas and dust about 1,344 light-years off. People call it a stellar nursery because they think stars form inside it. Nobody has watched one form.","more":"The distance is one of very few outside the solar system that is straight geometry. Radio telescopes spread across the United States measured the parallax of stars sitting inside the nebula and got 1,344 light-years, give or take 20. What is in the cloud has been measured. What is happening in it has not.","tryit":"On a winter evening find the three belt stars and look at the short line hanging below them, Orion's sword. The middle star of the sword is not a star. Through binoculars it opens out into a gray-green cloud with a knot of light in the center."},{"id":"deepsky-10","section":"deepsky","cat":"travel","kind":"record","text":"Go far enough south and you can see two fuzzy patches in the night sky. They look like torn-off pieces of the Milky Way lying on their own in the dark. You do not need a telescope. They are called the Magellanic Clouds.","more":"Sailors on Magellan's ships wrote them down in 1519, which is where the name comes from. People living under southern skies had been watching them long before that. The bigger of the two is where the exploding star of 1987 appeared."},{"id":"deepsky-11","section":"deepsky","cat":"build","kind":"record","text":"Nebula is just Latin for cloud, and for a long time it meant any fuzzy thing in a telescope. Then better instruments took the list apart. Some nebulae turned out to be swarms of stars too close together to separate. Others really are clouds of gas. They had been filed together for looking alike.","more":"Lord Rosse had a 72-inch telescope in Ireland. In 1845 it showed a spiral shape inside M51. Nobody had seen that structure in one of these patches before. Until then the Pleiades, the Andromeda galaxy and the Orion Nebula all sat on the same fuzzy-patch lists. Nobody could tell them apart."},{"id":"deepsky-12","section":"deepsky","cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"Photographs of nebulae are full of color. Through a telescope they are gray. Your eye has two kinds of light sensor, and the one that works in the dark cannot see color at all. A camera can sit collecting light for an hour; your eye starts over roughly every tenth of a second.","more":"The Orion Nebula is bright enough to push a hint of green through to some people. Observers have argued for a long time about who can actually see it. Either way it is not a fault in the telescope. It is how eyes are built."},{"id":"deepsky-13","section":"deepsky","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Right in the middle of the Orion Nebula, a small telescope shows four points of light in a tight lopsided box. They are called the Trapezium. A bigger telescope pulls two fainter ones out of the same small space. In binoculars all four blur into a single fuzzy dot.","more":"The cloud is brightest close to those stars and dimmer further out. That is why their light is taken to be the cause. Nobody has watched the lighting happen. What has been measured is that the brightness falls off with distance exactly as it should if the stars are the source. Any telescope at all splits the fuzzy spot into four sharp stars in a wisp of gray cloud."},{"id":"deepsky-14","section":"deepsky","cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Job 38:31 asks, \"Can you bind the chains of the Pleiades or loose the cords of Orion?\" Both are still up there and both still carry those names. You can go outside on a winter night and look at the two things the question is about. Very few old books can be checked that way.","more":"Job 9:9 and Amos 5:8 name the same pair. Whoever wrote them was not describing something abstract. The Pleiades is a small knot of stars in Taurus. Orion is the brightest, most obvious figure in the winter sky. Neither one has moved."},{"id":"deepsky-2","section":"deepsky","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"The Pleiades look like a tiny dipper rather than a fuzzy blob. Most people pick out six or seven stars in it. Binoculars turn that into dozens. The cluster sits about 444 light-years away. That number is measured, from the parallax of individual member stars. The Gaia spacecraft took those readings.","more":"The cluster has more than a thousand stars in it. Almost every culture with a clear sky has noticed it and given it a name. It is not hard to see why: a small tight knot of blue-white stars in an otherwise empty patch of sky.","tryit":"Look east on an fall evening for a small misty smudge of stars. Count how many you can separate with just your eyes. Then raise binoculars and count again. Six or seven usually becomes forty or more."},{"id":"deepsky-3","section":"deepsky","cat":"size","kind":"worked","text":"The Andromeda galaxy is the most distant thing a person can see without equipment. From a dark site it is a faint oval several full moons long. Its distance is put at about 2.5 million light-years, and that figure is worked out rather than measured with geometry.","more":"It comes from a kind of star whose true output is taken to be tied to how fast it pulses. Compare how bright those stars look in Andromeda with how bright they are assumed to be, and the difference gives a distance. Edwin Hubble did it first in 1923 using the 100-inch telescope at Mount Wilson, and his answer then was about a third of today's.","tryit":"In fall find the Great Square of Pegasus, then the two chains of stars trailing off its top left corner. Andromeda is a faint oval near the second star of the upper chain. Look slightly to one side of it and it brightens, which is a real trick and not your imagination."},{"id":"deepsky-4","section":"deepsky","cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"The Milky Way looks like a band rather than a spiral because we are inside it, looking along the flat part from within. On a dark night it runs right across the sky like something spilled. It is thickest and brightest toward Sagittarius, which is the direction of the middle.","more":"You are looking through more stars when you look along the disc than when you look out of it, which is the whole reason the band exists. It is not a cloud. Galileo settled that in 1610 by pointing a telescope at it and watching the glow break into separate stars. To see it you need a moonless night away from town and twenty minutes without looking at a phone; then face south in summer."},{"id":"deepsky-5","section":"deepsky","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"For a very long time nobody knew what the Milky Way was made of. In 1610 Galileo turned his small telescope on it and the glow broke apart into individual stars, each too faint to see on its own. He wrote it up in a short book, Sidereus Nuncius, published that March.","more":"The same book reported mountains and craters on the moon. It also reported four points of light shuffling around Jupiter. He sketched their positions on 64 separate nights, between 7 January and 2 March 1610. Only then was he willing to publish."},{"id":"deepsky-6","section":"deepsky","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"A globular cluster is a ball of stars packed so tightly the middle looks solid. M13, in Hercules, holds somewhere between a hundred thousand and six hundred thousand stars. Halley found it in 1714 and wrote that it shows itself to the naked eye when the sky is clear and the moon away.","more":"In a small telescope the edge of the ball crumbles into separate points. The center stays a solid glow. That is a real thing you are seeing, not a limit of the instrument. The stars near the middle really are crowded far closer together than the ones around here. To find it, look for the four stars of the Keystone in Hercules. M13 sits on the western side of that shape, about a third of the way down."},{"id":"deepsky-7","section":"deepsky","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Charles Messier hunted comets, and he kept getting fooled by fuzzy patches that turned out to sit still. So he wrote a list of them, the things not to waste a night on. That annoyed list is now the most used catalogue in amateur astronomy: 110 objects, M1 to M110, every one worth looking at.","more":"M1 is the Crab Nebula. M13 is the Hercules cluster. M31 is Andromeda. M42 is the Orion Nebula. M45 is the Pleiades. Messier built the bulk of the list between 1758 and 1782 and reached 103 entries. Other people added the last seven afterward, from his notes."},{"id":"deepsky-8","section":"deepsky","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"In December 1995 Hubble was aimed at a scrap of sky chosen because it looked empty, a spot about the apparent width of a dime seen from 75 feet. It stared at that nothing for ten days and took 342 exposures. The empty patch came back with roughly 3,000 galaxies in it.","more":"The patch covers about one twenty-four-millionth of the whole sky. Spending ten days of a very expensive telescope on a blank spot was a genuine gamble. The director of the institute had to spend his own discretionary observing time to make it happen."},{"id":"deepsky-9","section":"deepsky","cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"Some of the shapes in the sky are holes. The Coalsack, beside the Southern Cross, is a place where the Milky Way simply stops: a cloud of dust in the way, blocking everything behind it. Aboriginal Australians mapped it as the head of an emu whose body stretches on toward Scorpius.","more":"Western constellations are made of bright stars. That tradition is made of the dark gaps between them, which needs a properly dark sky to see at all. The Coalsack is one of the few dark nebulae obvious to the unaided eye, and it is about 600 light-years off."},{"id":"moon-1","section":"moon","cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"Six Apollo crews landed on the moon and carried back 842 pounds (382 kg) of rock, dust and drilled cores, about 2,200 separate samples. They are kept in Houston. Pieces are still being cut off and sent to laboratories. This is moon that people have held in their hands.","more":"Some of it has deliberately never been opened. A few sealed containers were left shut on purpose. Later labs would have better tools. Leaving those containers shut gave them untouched material to work on. Sample tubes sealed on the moon were opened for the first time decades later, exactly as planned."},{"id":"moon-10","section":"moon","cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"There is no air on the moon to move heat around, so the difference between sunlight and shade is brutal. Near the equator the ground reaches about 250 °F (121 °C) in the sun and drops to around -207 °F (-133 °C) in the dark. Inside some polar craters sunlight never arrives at all.","more":"NASA's Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter has measured spots in those permanently shadowed craters below -410 °F (-246 °C). They are among the coldest places ever measured anywhere. On Earth, air spreads heat around and softens the swing. Step from sun into shadow on the moon and there is nothing to soften it."},{"id":"moon-11","section":"moon","cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"The bootprints Apollo left are still there. No wind, no rain, nothing to sweep them away. Only slow dusting from tiny impacts will wear them down. The Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter has photographed the landing sites from orbit, and the tracks the crews walked and drove still show up."},{"id":"moon-12","section":"moon","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"The moon is not only a night-time object. For most of the month it is up during daylight, pale blue-white against the blue sky. Most people never notice it, for the simple reason that they never look.","tryit":"In the week after a full moon, look west in the morning. In the week before a full moon, look east in the late afternoon. Find it in daylight once and you will start seeing it constantly."},{"id":"moon-13","section":"moon","cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"The worst time to look at craters is full moon. The sun is straight overhead there, nothing casts a shadow, and the surface looks flat and washed out. Along the terminator, the line dividing lit from unlit, the sun sits low and every crater rim throws a long shadow.","tryit":"Rest binoculars on a fence or a windowsill so they stop shaking, and look along the terminator a few nights after new moon. The craters stand up in relief. Look at the same craters at full moon and they nearly disappear."},{"id":"moon-14","section":"moon","cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis puts the lesser light in charge of the night, set alongside the sun and the stars for signs and seasons. The moon does keep time. It runs from new to full and back to new in about 29 and a half days, and that is where the month came from."},{"id":"moon-2","section":"moon","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"The moon keeps one face toward us, but it does not hold it perfectly still. It rocks slightly north and south and wobbles east and west as it goes round, so over time 59% of its surface comes into view. Not half. A bit more than half. The wobble is called libration.","more":"Two things cause it. The moon's orbit is not a perfect circle, so it speeds up and slows down. Its spin stays steady the whole time. That lets us peek a little way round each side. Its axis is also tilted about 6.7 degrees. So we see a little over the top and a little under the bottom."},{"id":"moon-3","section":"moon","cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"Nobody had seen the far side of the moon until 7 October 1959. On that day the Soviet probe Luna 3 looped behind it and took 29 photographs. The film was developed on board, scanned, and radioed home. The pictures were grainy. They showed something nobody expected: almost none of the big dark plains.","more":"The near side is blotched with dark flat areas called maria, which is Latin for seas, though there is no water in them. The far side is nearly all crater. Whatever the reason, the two hemispheres do not match, and one blurry set of photographs from 1959 is what first showed it."},{"id":"moon-4","section":"moon","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"The far side is not the dark side. It gets just as much sunlight as the side we see. It simply never faces us. When the moon is new and looks completely black from Earth, the far side is in full daylight."},{"id":"moon-5","section":"moon","cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"Apollo 11, 14 and 15 left panels of mirrored corner-cubes sitting on the moon. Observatories still fire lasers at them and time the round trip. The measurement is good to a few millimeters. It shows the moon drifting away at about 1.5 inches (3.8 cm) each year. That is roughly the speed a fingernail grows.","more":"A corner-cube is a clever shape: whatever angle light comes in at, it goes back out the way it came. That is why the mirrors do not need aiming. Apollo 15's array is the biggest and still gives the strongest return. It is the longest-running experiment ever left on another world."},{"id":"moon-6","section":"moon","cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"Nobody has drilled into the moon. What gets said about its middle is worked out from two things Apollo left behind. Seismometers recorded the ground shaking. Mirrors let lasers time the moon's wobble. A partly liquid core wobbles differently from a solid one. Reasoned, not seen.","more":"A body with a small liquid center does not swing quite like one that is solid all the way through. The laser mirrors measure that swing precisely enough to tell the difference. It is a good measurement pointing at a conclusion. It is not a look inside."},{"id":"moon-7","section":"moon","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Apollo crews left seismometers switched on. Between 1972 and 1977 those instruments recorded 28 shallow moonquakes. The strongest measured about 5.5. Some kept shaking for more than ten minutes, far longer than an earthquake of that size. Dry cracked rock with no water in it rings on instead of settling down."},{"id":"moon-8","section":"moon","cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"Moon dust got tracked into the lunar module on every landing. Charlie Duke and Harrison Schmitt both reported the same thing once their helmets were off: it smelled like spent gunpowder. Schmitt pointed out that all the crews had fired guns and knew the smell. It faded before the samples reached Earth.","more":"The dust itself was a genuine nuisance. It is sharp and clingy, because there is no wind or water to round it off. It scratched visors, wore out seals, and left Schmitt sneezing with what he called lunar hay fever. No one has fully pinned down what made the smell."},{"id":"moon-9","section":"moon","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"The moon is 2,159 miles across (3,475 km), a bit over a quarter of Earth's width. It sits an average of 238,855 miles away (384,400 km), about thirty Earths laid side by side. That distance is not an estimate. Lasers measure it on clear nights."},{"id":"looking-1","section":"looking","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Galileo's telescope was worse than a decent pair of binoculars. It magnified about twenty times and showed a keyhole of sky at a time. With that he found four points of light beside Jupiter that swapped places night after night, mountains on the moon, and Venus running through phases.","more":"He published in March 1610, only weeks after the observations, which was unusually fast for the time. The four moons he called the Medicean Stars, after his patrons. Everybody else called them the Galilean moons, and that is the name that survived.","tryit":"Brace a pair of binoculars against a fence or door frame and look at Jupiter. Three or four tiny dots strung out in a line beside it are the same moons Galileo saw. Look again the next night and they will have shuffled."},{"id":"looking-10","section":"looking","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"Your eyes take half an hour to switch over to night mode, and one glance at a phone undoes it. The sensors that work in the dark rely on a pigment that takes time to rebuild, and bright light bleaches it out in a second. Red light barely touches it, so observers use red flashlights.","tryit":"Go outside, sit down, and do not look at any light at all for thirty minutes. Then look up. The same sky you glanced at on the way out will hold three or four times as many stars. You will not have changed anything but your own eyes."},{"id":"looking-11","section":"looking","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The best first instrument is not a telescope. A pair of 10x50 binoculars costs a fraction as much and needs no setting up. It gives both eyes the same view. Brace your elbows on something and it stays steady enough. Nearly everything worth seeing in a night sky is within their reach.","more":"The two numbers mean ten times magnification and 50-millimeter (2-inch) front lenses. The second number matters more, because it is how much light gets in. Anything above about 10x is hard to hold still, which is why 7x50 and 10x50 are the two sizes astronomers keep recommending."},{"id":"looking-12","section":"looking","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"A telescope's real job is collecting light, not magnifying. A 6-inch mirror gathers around 500 times as much light as a fully dark-adapted pupil. That is why it shows things the eye cannot reach at all. Magnification is a separate setting entirely. You change it by swapping the eyepiece.","more":"Advertised magnifications like 525x on cheap telescopes are close to meaningless. Push a small instrument too far and you get a big, dim, mushy blob. The useful ceiling is roughly 50x per inch of aperture. Unsteady air normally stops you well below that anyway."},{"id":"looking-13","section":"looking","cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"Big telescopes end up in awkward places for one reason: they need still, dry, dark air above them. Mauna Kea in Hawaii sits at 13,800 feet, high enough to make people ill. The Atacama in Chile is one of the driest deserts on Earth. Both are miserable to work at. Both give sharper pictures.","more":"Water vapor in the air soaks up infrared. So a dry site sees colors a wet one cannot. Height gets you above part of the atmosphere and above most of the turbulence. Nobody builds a major observatory somewhere convenient. Convenient places have weather, water and streetlights."},{"id":"looking-14","section":"looking","cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis 1:14 puts the lights in the sky \"for signs and for seasons, and for days and years\". Think of Galileo's little tube, the 200-inch Hale telescope, and Webb's folded gold mirror. Every instrument ever built for the sky is an extension of one plain activity. Going outside and looking up carefully.","more":"The oldest working instrument is still the naked eye. It is not retired. Bessel's parallax, Goodricke's timing of Algol and the Chinese record of the guest star of 1054 all came from watching. So did every constellation ever named. Nothing but eyes, patience and somewhere to write it down."},{"id":"looking-2","section":"looking","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"William Herschel was a working musician who ground his own mirrors. On 13 March 1781 he was sweeping through the sky from his garden in Bath. He found a small disk that was not a star. He reported it as a comet. It turned out to be a planet, the first ever found with a telescope.","more":"Herschel wanted to call it Georgium Sidus after King George III. Other astronomers preferred Uranus, and Uranus won. The instrument was a reflector with a 6.2-inch mirror in a tube about seven feet long, made at home by hand."},{"id":"looking-3","section":"looking","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"The 200-inch Hale telescope on Palomar Mountain opened in 1948. It stayed the most capable telescope on Earth for 45 years. Its mirror is a single disk of Pyrex, over 16 feet across and weighing 14.5 tons. A honeycomb pattern is cast into the back to take weight out of it.","more":"It is still working. Casting that disc at Corning took years and the first attempt failed outright. Every larger telescope built since has gone a different way: either a much thinner mirror held in shape by motors, or a mosaic of smaller ones."},{"id":"looking-4","section":"looking","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The two Keck telescopes in Hawaii each carry a 33-foot (10 m) mirror that is not one mirror. It is 36 hexagons, held in line by motors that adjust them twice a second. A second, flexible mirror further down the light path bends hundreds of times a second to undo the blurring of the air.","more":"To measure that blurring they fire a laser into the sky, lighting up sodium atoms about 56 miles up to make an artificial star exactly where they want one. The computer watches how the fake star wobbles and bends the flexible mirror to cancel the wobble out. Done well, the result beats Hubble for sharpness."},{"id":"looking-5","section":"looking","cat":"travel","kind":"record","text":"Hubble went up on 24 April 1990 with a 7.9-foot (2.4 m) mirror ground to the wrong shape. Its edge was too flat by about four microns, a fiftieth the thickness of a hair, and every picture came back soft. In December 1993 astronauts flew up and fitted corrective optics to it.","more":"The fix was called COSTAR, a set of small curved mirrors. They put exactly the opposite error into the light before it reached the instruments, like spectacles. It worked first time. Later instruments had the correction built into them. COSTAR was eventually taken out and put in a museum."},{"id":"looking-6","section":"looking","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Webb's mirror is 21 feet 4 inches (6.5 m) across and it folds. It is 18 hexagons of beryllium under a skin of gold, each steered by seven motors. It sits about a million miles from Earth behind a five-layer sunshield the size of a tennis court, and it looks in infrared.","more":"Infrared is heat, so the telescope has to be colder than anything it wants to look at. The sunshield holds a difference of roughly 570 degrees F between its hot side and its cold side. Nobody can fly out and fix it, so every one of the hundreds of unfolding steps after launch had to work first time. They did."},{"id":"looking-7","section":"looking","cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"Some telescopes have no lens and no eyepiece, because they catch radio waves instead of light. The Green Bank dish in West Virginia is 328 feet (100 m) across, covers over two acres, and steers to any part of the sky. What comes out is a measurement, not a picture.","more":"The 1,000-foot Arecibo dish in Puerto Rico was the largest single dish for over fifty years. Then its support cables gave way. The platform crashed into it on 1 December 2020. China's FAST is now the biggest, at 1,640 feet. It can only aim about 1,000 feet (300 m) of itself at a time."},{"id":"looking-8","section":"looking","cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"Voyager 1 launched on 5 September 1977 and has never stopped. On 14 February 1990, from 3.7 billion miles out, it turned its camera around and photographed Earth. In the picture our planet is a speck less than one pixel across, sitting in a band of scattered sunlight.","more":"Carl Sagan pushed for years to have that picture taken. There was no scientific reason for it and NASA repeatedly said no. The cameras were switched off permanently 34 minutes later to save power, and Voyager 1 is now the most distant object people have ever built."},{"id":"looking-9","section":"looking","cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"John Bortle published a nine-step scale in Sky & Telescope in February 2001 to grade how dark a site is. Class 1 is the darkest ground left on Earth, where the Milky Way can cast a shadow. Class 9 is an inner city: the moon, the planets and a handful of stars.","more":"Most suburbs land around class 5 or 6, where the Milky Way is a vague smear near the horizon or gone altogether. The difference between a class 5 sky and a class 3 sky is usually a forty-minute drive, and it is the single biggest upgrade available to any observer. To place your own sky: can you see the Milky Way at all, and can you find Andromeda without a chart? Count the stars inside the Great Square of Pegasus. Under a genuinely dark sky there are more than a dozen; from a city, none."},{"id":"smallstuff-1","section":"smallstuff","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Films show the asteroid belt as a shooting gallery you have to dodge. The real belt is mostly nothing. The asteroids in it work out to roughly 600,000 miles apart on average, more than twice the distance from Earth to the moon. Spacecraft fly straight through without steering around anything.","more":"Pioneer 10 went in first, entering the belt in July 1972 and coming out the far side in February 1973 with fewer dust hits than anyone expected. Pioneer 11, both Voyagers, Galileo, Cassini, Juno and New Horizons have all crossed since. Standing on one asteroid, you would be lucky to see a second one at all."},{"id":"smallstuff-10","section":"smallstuff","cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"A shooting star is usually a grain of sand. Most meteors come from specks smaller than a grain of rice, moving fast enough that the air ahead of them glows white. They burn out 50 to 75 miles (80 to 120 km) up, far above any aircraft, and the streak lasts under a second.","more":"The three words are worth keeping straight. The pebble out in space is a meteoroid. The streak of light is the meteor. A piece that survives and reaches the ground is a meteorite. Most meteorites in the world's collections were picked up in Antarctica. Dark rocks show plainly on blue ice, and the ice flow piles them up in a few spots. Over 35,000 have been recovered there."},{"id":"smallstuff-11","section":"smallstuff","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"The Perseids arrive the same week every August because Earth crosses the same trail of comet dust every year. The comet that left it is 109P/Swift-Tuttle. The peak is the night of 12 to 13 August, and at a properly dark site with no moon people count 50 to 100 an hour.","tryit":"Mark 12 August on the calendar. Get away from streetlights, look up and away from the moon, and start watching around 11 pm. The rate climbs through the night and is best in the couple of hours before dawn."},{"id":"smallstuff-12","section":"smallstuff","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"A comet's tail does not trail behind it like smoke off a train. It points away from the sun, always. Sunlight and the stream of particles blowing off the sun push the dust and gas straight outward. So a comet on its way back out of the solar system travels tail first.","more":"There are usually two tails, and they point slightly differently. The gas tail glows bluish and runs almost exactly away from the sun. The dust tail is broader, whiter and curved, because the dust grains keep some of the comet's own motion as they drift."},{"id":"smallstuff-13","section":"smallstuff","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"On 12 November 2014 a lander called Philae was dropped onto comet 67P. Its harpoons failed to fire. It bounced, drifted for nearly two hours in the comet's almost non-existent gravity, and came down again about half a mile away in shadow. It still worked for around 64 hours.","more":"It was the first landing on a comet, ever. The mother ship Rosetta had taken ten years to get there. Because Philae ended up out of the sunlight, its solar panels could not keep the batteries up, but it sent its measurements home before it went quiet."},{"id":"smallstuff-14","section":"smallstuff","cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis 1:14 says the lights in the sky were set there \"for signs and for seasons, and for days and years\". The small stuff keeps that kind of time too. Edmond Halley predicted his comet's return decades ahead, and it arrived. The Perseids turn up in the same week of every single August.","more":"This is what made Halley's prediction so convincing to people at the time. He was not describing a wonder; he was setting a date, publicly, for something nobody alive would check for 53 years. Then it turned up."},{"id":"smallstuff-2","section":"smallstuff","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"The largest thing in the asteroid belt is Ceres, about 585 miles (940 km) across. Giuseppe Piazzi found it on the first night of 1801. It was the very first asteroid anyone spotted. NASA's Dawn spacecraft arrived in 2015 and went into orbit around it. That made Ceres the first dwarf planet ever visited.","more":"Dawn photographed a crater called Occator with a startling bright patch in the middle. The patch is far brighter than the gray ground around it. Ceres is also counted as a dwarf planet. So it holds two titles at once. Biggest asteroid, and nearest dwarf planet to us."},{"id":"smallstuff-3","section":"smallstuff","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"On 24 August 2006 astronomers met in Prague and voted on what the word planet means. It goes round the sun. It is round itself. It has swept its own orbital lane clear. Pluto passes the first two and fails the third. So it was moved to a new category, dwarf planet.","more":"Nothing about Pluto changed that day. The definition did. Pluto shares its region of the solar system with Eris and a crowd of other bodies of similar size. That is exactly what failing to clear your neighborhood looks like. Plenty of astronomers still call the rule a bad one."},{"id":"smallstuff-4","section":"smallstuff","cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"New Horizons had one shot at Pluto. On 14 July 2015 it went past at around 32,000 miles an hour, 7,800 miles (12,500 km) above the surface, with less than half an hour to photograph the whole world. The pictures came back showing a pale heart and a range of mountains.","more":"The heart is named Tombaugh Regio, after Clyde Tombaugh, who found Pluto in 1930. Its left half, Sputnik Planitia, is a 600-mile plain of frozen nitrogen without a single crater on it. The mountains beside it stand about 11,000 feet (3,400 m) high and are made of water ice, which at Pluto's temperature is as hard as rock."},{"id":"smallstuff-5","section":"smallstuff","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"The largest meteorite anyone has found is still lying exactly where it landed. Hoba, in Namibia, weighs over 60 tons and measures about 9 feet by 9 feet by 3 feet thick. A farmer's plow struck it in 1920. Nobody has ever moved it, because nobody can.","more":"It is a slab of metal, roughly 84 percent iron and 16 percent nickel with a trace of cobalt. Visitors and collectors have chipped pieces off over the years, so it weighs a little less now than when it was found. It sits in an open stone amphitheatre and you can walk up and put your hand flat on it."},{"id":"smallstuff-6","section":"smallstuff","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"On 15 February 2013 a rock about 60 feet (18 m) across came in over Chelyabinsk in Russia and blew apart roughly 18 miles up. Nothing hit the city; the shock wave did. It blew in windows across a wide strip of ground and cut about 1,500 people with flying glass.","more":"Nobody was killed. The airburst released energy on the order of 400 to 600 kilotons of TNT. That makes it the largest such event over a populated place in living memory. Russian drivers commonly run dashboard cameras. The whole thing was filmed from dozens of angles at once, and can be watched frame by frame."},{"id":"smallstuff-7","section":"smallstuff","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Edmond Halley noticed in 1705 that bright comets seen in 1531, 1607 and 1682 all followed the same path, and said it was one comet coming back. He predicted the next return for 1758. It arrived, sixteen years after he died. Chinese astronomers had logged the same comet as far back as 240 BC.","more":"It came again in 1835, 1910 and 1986. It is due on 28 July 2061. The 1066 visit is stitched into the Bayeux Tapestry, with a row of men pointing up at it. In 1986 the European probe Giotto flew close enough to photograph the solid lump at the center. It turned out to be a very dark peanut-shaped thing about nine miles long."},{"id":"smallstuff-8","section":"smallstuff","cat":"travel","kind":"found","text":"OSIRIS-REx flew to the asteroid Bennu, touched it for a few seconds, grabbed a handful and came home. The capsule landed in the Utah desert on 24 September 2023 with 4.29 ounces (121.6 g) of Bennu inside. People can now hold pieces of an asteroid in a laboratory.","more":"The collecting arm scooped up so much material that a flap jammed open. Grains began drifting away into space. The team stowed the sample early rather than lose more. The mission needed 60 grams to count as a success. It came back with twice that."},{"id":"smallstuff-9","section":"smallstuff","cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"Japan brought asteroid samples home before NASA's OSIRIS-REx did. Hayabusa2 spent about eighteen months at the asteroid Ryugu. It fired a copper slug into the asteroid to expose material from below the surface. It dropped a capsule into the South Australian desert on 6 December 2020. Inside were 5.4 grams of Ryugu, roughly a teaspoon.","more":"A helicopter crew tracked the capsule down by its radio beacon. They found it sitting intact in the sand at Woomera. Nobody had ever collected material from beneath an asteroid's surface before, as well as from the top. That matters, because the top has been sitting out in the open."}];
globalThis.ASTRO = ASTRO;
